---
deadlock_detection: false
---
# Model #6 — scheduler cancel-responsiveness (STPA CA1, DR-1/DR-9).
#
# Seam:
#   crates/gh-report/src/app/daemon.rs::next_collection_tick (:119-132) —
#     `tokio::select! { biased; _ = cancel.changed() => Cancel, () =
#     sleep(interval) => Run }`, checked BEFORE `sleep` too
#     (`if *cancel.borrow() { return Cancel }`). This is the sole gate
#     between one collection cycle finishing and the next one starting.
#   crates/gh-report/src/app/daemon.rs::spawn_collection_loop (:653-745)
#     — the `loop { match next_collection_tick(...) { Cancel => return,
#     Run => {} } ... }` shape: regardless of whether the PRIOR cycle's
#     collect::run_with_outcome returned Completed, Cancelled,
#     FencedConflict, or an Err, control always falls back through to
#     next_collection_tick at the top of the loop (:663-741 — every
#     match arm falls through, none early-returns except Cancel).
#
# ADRs: none dedicated to this constant/loop shape (a gap the STPA
#       analysis already flagged for DR-7's sibling constant,
#       SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_TIMEOUT — this model documents the analogous
#       CA1 gap: no ADR governs the scheduler's cancel-check cadence
#       either).
#
# STPA hazard modeled: CA1-NP ("Tick does not fire ... task panicked")
# and the H3 stall it causes are, per DR-1's own ICP note, only
# detectable from OUTSIDE the loop (an external staleness monitor) —
# nothing in-band can prove a genuinely-hung task will resume. What CAN
# be verified in-band, and is the actual claim this model makes, is
# narrower and load-bearing: GIVEN the loop is still alive (not
# panicked), does it always eventually honor a cancel request, no
# matter which outcome the previous collection cycle produced? A
# regression that special-cases one outcome branch to skip the
# next_collection_tick check (e.g. an early `continue` added ahead of
# it) would silently reintroduce H3 for that branch alone — exactly the
# class of bug a formal model catches that a happy-path integration
# test would not.

MAX_TICKS = 3
# Bound on ticks_run, matching the other specs' finite-state-space
# convention (NUM_WRITERS, TICKS, etc.) — the real loop runs
# unboundedly, but a cancel request that never arrives within a few
# iterations is not the property under test here; this bound keeps the
# reachable state space finite without weakening the assertions below.

atomic fair action RequestCancel:
    require not cancel_requested
    cancel_requested = True

# Models one full loop iteration's gate: check cancel_requested (as
# next_collection_tick does, both before AND via the biased select),
# and either exit the loop (Cancel) or advance to another tick (Run).
# `ticks_run` stands in for "collect::run_with_outcome executed once",
# whose actual Ok/Err variant does not affect whether this gate re-fires
# on the NEXT iteration — that fall-through-regardless-of-outcome shape
# is exactly what daemon.rs:663-741's match arms already guarantee and
# this model exists to hold constant under future edits.
atomic fair action NextTickCheck:
    require not loop_exited
    if cancel_requested:
        loop_exited = True
    elif ticks_run < MAX_TICKS:
        ticks_run = ticks_run + 1

action Init:
    cancel_requested = False
    loop_exited = False
    ticks_run = 0

# SAFETY: the loop never exits before a cancel was actually requested —
# no spurious/early exit (would itself be a different hazard: dropping
# a scheduled collection cycle nobody asked to cancel).
always assertion NoSpuriousExit:
    if loop_exited:
        return cancel_requested
    return True

# LIVENESS: once cancel is requested, the loop always eventually exits —
# NextTickCheck's biased, always-reachable gate re-fires every
# iteration regardless of the prior tick's collection outcome. This is
# the formal counterpart of "the scheduler is never blocked mid-loop
# unable to observe shutdown", complementing DR-1's out-of-band
# staleness monitor (which catches the orthogonal panicked/no-tick-at-
# all case this model does not attempt to model in-band).
eventually always assertion CancelAlwaysEventuallyHonored:
    if cancel_requested:
        return loop_exited
    return True

# --- TEETH (falsification) ---
# To reproduce a lost-cancel regression, invert NextTickCheck's guard —
# models a bug where the cancel flag is read but the branch is backwards
# (an easy transcription error when refactoring the `match` arms):
#
#     if cancel_requested:
#         ticks_run = ticks_run + 1   # BUG: keeps ticking on cancel
#     else:
#         loop_exited = True          # BUG: exits when nobody asked
#
# With that change, CancelAlwaysEventuallyHonored fails: once
# cancel_requested is True, the (buggy) branch keeps advancing
# ticks_run forever instead of setting loop_exited, so the assertion's
# `return loop_exited` is never satisfied. NoSpuriousExit also fails
# independently (the loop can exit with cancel_requested still False).
# See README.md for the captured fizz counterexample output.
